WEBVTT

00:00:00.618 --> 00:00:01.550
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.711 --> 00:00:04.110
Today we talk about French and ombré.

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:07.390
These are two nail styles that never go out of fashion:

00:00:07.579 --> 00:00:12.990
elegant, clean, and right for almost every client, from brides to office workers.

00:00:13.338 --> 00:00:15.575
But precisely because they are so simple,

00:00:15.525 --> 00:00:18.990
even a small mistake is something the client sees straight away.

00:00:19.718 --> 00:00:21.639
Let me tell you about something very common.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.599
A technician paints French very skillfully.

00:00:24.912 --> 00:00:27.718
Looked at one by one, every nail is beautiful.

00:00:27.885 --> 00:00:32.766
But when the client puts both hands on the table, this nail has a deep white tip,

00:00:32.716 --> 00:00:33.959
that one is shallow,

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.759
this one is balanced, that one is lopsided.

00:00:37.043 --> 00:00:40.519
The client says nothing, but next time, she does not come back.

00:00:40.928 --> 00:00:44.119
With French, the beauty is not in a single nail.

00:00:44.266 --> 00:00:46.999
It is in all ten nails, side by side.

00:00:47.645 --> 00:00:50.448
The soul of a French set is the smile line.

00:00:50.866 --> 00:00:53.328
That is the curve where the white meets the pink.

00:00:53.986 --> 00:00:56.608
A beautiful smile line has four qualities.

00:00:56.947 --> 00:00:58.688
One, an even depth.

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:00.528
Two, balance:

00:01:00.797 --> 00:01:03.088
the upper and lower halves mirror each other.

00:01:03.437 --> 00:01:07.168
Three, a crisp, clean edge, with no blurring.

00:01:07.437 --> 00:01:08.288
And four:

00:01:08.474 --> 00:01:10.528
the same on all ten nails.

00:01:11.053 --> 00:01:12.846
So where should the smile line go?

00:01:13.237 --> 00:01:17.116
The most natural way is to follow the natural free edge line of the client,

00:01:17.066 --> 00:01:19.566
where the nail leaves the skin of the fingertip.

00:01:19.915 --> 00:01:22.206
But that line differs from finger to finger:

00:01:22.396 --> 00:01:26.366
some curve deeply, some are almost straight, some are crooked.

00:01:26.712 --> 00:01:29.326
In that case, we do not follow the crookedness.

00:01:29.494 --> 00:01:33.424
We choose one beautiful smile line that suits the whole hand,

00:01:33.374 --> 00:01:36.286
and paint exactly that line on all ten nails.

00:01:36.692 --> 00:01:39.394
Where the natural free edge underneath is crooked,

00:01:39.344 --> 00:01:42.046
we cover it with a slightly more opaque pink base.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.222
And how deep should the white be?

00:01:44.545 --> 00:01:45.822
There is no fixed number.

00:01:46.201 --> 00:01:49.662
The principle is to balance it with the length and shape of the nail.

00:01:50.011 --> 00:01:54.782
On short nails, keep the white slim, and the fingers will look slimmer and longer.

00:01:55.131 --> 00:01:57.262
On long nails, you can go deeper.

00:01:57.644 --> 00:02:01.822
White that is too deep on short nails makes the fingers look short and stubby.

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:04.622
The nail shape also decides the smile line:

00:02:04.854 --> 00:02:07.077
square nails take a flatter smile line,

00:02:07.027 --> 00:02:10.142
and almond nails a deeper curve that follows the shape.

00:02:10.565 --> 00:02:13.182
For nail shapes, review the nail shaping lesson.

00:02:13.804 --> 00:02:16.458
From there, we get several kinds of French.

00:02:16.838 --> 00:02:17.578
Classic:

00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:20.538
a sheer pink base and a moderate white tip.

00:02:21.089 --> 00:02:21.658
Deep:

00:02:22.032 --> 00:02:26.058
a higher white, with a more pronounced curve, which suits long nails.

00:02:26.472 --> 00:02:27.098
Micro:

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:31.118
just a hairline of white at the edge of the tip, perfect for short nails,

00:02:31.068 --> 00:02:33.498
or for clients who want something very discreet.

00:02:33.837 --> 00:02:34.858
And colored French:

00:02:34.962 --> 00:02:38.736
the white replaced with another color, burgundy, gold glitter,

00:02:38.686 --> 00:02:40.538
or a shade to match an outfit.

00:02:40.804 --> 00:02:42.698
The technique is exactly the same;

00:02:42.773 --> 00:02:43.978
only the color changes.

00:02:44.547 --> 00:02:47.344
As for ombré, it is also called baby boomer.

00:02:47.756 --> 00:02:48.864
It is a color fade:

00:02:49.037 --> 00:02:52.704
natural pink at the cuticle, fading into milky white at the tip.

00:02:53.092 --> 00:02:54.704
There is no smile line here.

00:02:55.211 --> 00:02:58.864
The beauty lies in not being able to see where the color starts to change.

00:02:59.217 --> 00:03:00.464
Like a thin mist.

00:03:00.854 --> 00:03:04.139
For clients who want their nails to look natural for a long time,

00:03:04.089 --> 00:03:05.184
it is a great choice:

00:03:05.366 --> 00:03:09.636
as the nail grows, the new growth at the cuticle blends into the pink,

00:03:09.586 --> 00:03:11.264
so there is no obvious gap,

00:03:11.374 --> 00:03:12.464
like with a dark color.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.501
To get that mist, there are two secrets.

00:03:15.766 --> 00:03:16.381
One:

00:03:16.561 --> 00:03:17.981
very thin layers.

00:03:18.232 --> 00:03:22.263
When the white layer is thin, the pink underneath still shows through,

00:03:22.213 --> 00:03:23.741
so the color fades softly.

00:03:24.077 --> 00:03:24.541
Two:

00:03:24.857 --> 00:03:26.141
build it up gradually.

00:03:26.389 --> 00:03:30.701
Blend lightly, in several rounds, rather than trying to finish in one go.

00:03:31.041 --> 00:03:32.221
Something that happens a lot:

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:36.941
a technician in a hurry puts on one thick layer of white, then tries to blend it.

00:03:37.179 --> 00:03:42.141
The result is a white band across the nail, patchy, as if paint had been smeared on.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.022
Now let us go over the common mistakes.

00:03:45.337 --> 00:03:46.302
The first mistake:

00:03:46.485 --> 00:03:50.622
uneven depth, between the two sides of a nail, or between nails.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:51.982
The second mistake:

00:03:52.258 --> 00:03:53.902
white that is too thick.

00:03:54.248 --> 00:03:58.650
White gel is very opaque, so the light of the lamp struggles to get through,

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.742
and a thick layer easily stays under cured underneath,

00:04:01.854 --> 00:04:03.502
wrinkles, and lifts.

00:04:03.888 --> 00:04:04.942
The third mistake:

00:04:05.047 --> 00:04:09.262
a blurred edge, because the smile line was not cleaned up before curing.

00:04:09.848 --> 00:04:10.942
And the fourth mistake:

00:04:11.231 --> 00:04:15.902
white that is too chalky, or streaky, from trying to cover in a single layer,

00:04:15.852 --> 00:04:18.302
or because the gel was not stirred well.

00:04:18.924 --> 00:04:22.062
You heard this in the gel polish lesson on making it last:

00:04:22.411 --> 00:04:27.021
thin layers cure evenly, thick layers wrinkle and do not cure all the way through.

00:04:27.441 --> 00:04:29.502
With white, this is even more true.

00:04:29.856 --> 00:04:33.929
Two thin layers of white, each cured for the full time the brand gives,

00:04:33.879 --> 00:04:35.742
are better than one thick layer.

00:04:36.092 --> 00:04:40.062
Under cured gel lifts easily, and is more likely to cause allergies.

00:04:40.324 --> 00:04:42.702
And uncured gel must never touch the skin.

00:04:43.366 --> 00:04:46.146
As for products, no special brand is needed.

00:04:46.567 --> 00:04:49.975
You need a white gel for the tips, thick enough not to run,

00:04:49.925 --> 00:04:53.106
and opaque enough to be even in one or two thin layers.

00:04:53.535 --> 00:04:57.026
A pink or nude base gel, sheer or slightly opaque.

00:04:57.588 --> 00:05:01.398
Many technicians use white and nude builder gel to paint the tips,

00:05:01.348 --> 00:05:03.506
because it is thick, holds its shape,

00:05:03.615 --> 00:05:04.866
and is easy to control.

00:05:05.126 --> 00:05:05.986
For tools:

00:05:06.152 --> 00:05:09.430
a long, thin liner brush to paint the smile line,

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.066
a small flat brush to clean up the line,

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:16.626
and for ombré, a small sponge, or a clean brush, for blending.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.826
Curing times always follow the instructions of the brand.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:21.992
Finally, the client.

00:05:22.328 --> 00:05:23.832
Some clients bring a photo:

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.552
long nails, a deep smile line.

00:05:27.053 --> 00:05:30.312
But her natural nails are short, and the free edge is crooked.

00:05:30.728 --> 00:05:31.432
Be honest:

00:05:31.781 --> 00:05:35.032
on these nails, that style will make the fingers look shorter.

00:05:35.461 --> 00:05:39.592
I would suggest a micro French, a slimmer classic, or an ombré.

00:05:40.022 --> 00:05:42.712
Explain why, and show her on her own nails.

00:05:42.871 --> 00:05:46.888
Clients are usually very grateful, because they see you are doing it for them,

00:05:46.838 --> 00:05:48.312
and not just to get it done.

00:05:48.951 --> 00:05:51.591
You see, French and ombré look simple.

00:05:52.059 --> 00:05:54.871
But whether they are beautiful comes down to consistency:

00:05:55.133 --> 00:05:59.431
the same depth, the same thinness, the same on all ten nails.

00:05:59.764 --> 00:06:02.151
Once you understand it, your hands will follow.

00:06:02.488 --> 00:06:05.671
In the practice lesson, we will go step by step together.

00:06:05.938 --> 00:06:08.551
And now, take the short quiz below the video.

00:06:08.722 --> 00:06:09.591
See you next time.
